# CLOSURE-ESCAPE CAPTURE AXIS, cell (param × literal). See
# `closure_escape_capture_axis_param_named.gg` for the full 2×2.
#
# `make_adder` takes `String p`, builds a closure capturing it, and RETURNS the
# closure. `p` is a parameter, so it binds a BORROW — the caller keeps
# ownership — and the environment outlives `make_adder`'s frame by construction.
# The capture is an ownership boundary, so a live borrow clones: the escaping
# environment holds its own `String`, and `make_adder`'s scope exit frees only
# the caller's value.
#
# Without the rule the environment stored the borrowed handle:
#
#     freed by:   gorget_string_free  ← make_adder  ← main
#     read by:    gorget_str_cat      ← mk          ← __Closure_0__call
#
# ⚠ NEITHER MUTATION NOR A COLLECTION IS NEEDED. The sibling cells trigger
# through a realloc of a captured collection; this one needs only the closure
# outliving its frame via `return`. The deallocation is the defining function's
# own scope exit.
#
# ⚠ AND THE CAPTURE NEED NOT BE A PARAMETER — that claim was false. A captured
# LOCAL escapes identically (`closure_escape_capture_axis_local_literal.gg`);
# what used to save the local case was the SPELLING, since
# `check_expr_for_escaping_closures` never inspects a returned closure literal.
# The control that IS meaningful is `Callable[String()] make(): return ():
# mk("hel","lo")` — same escape, no capture — which was always clean.
#
# ⚠ A plain build was rc 0 with SILENTLY WRONG OUTPUT, which is worse than the
# crash the collection cells give; `--sanitize` reports
# `AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free`.
#
# Prints `hello`, rc 0, both backends, ASan-clean.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

Callable[String()] make_adder(String p):
    return (): mk(p, "lo")

void main():
    Callable[String()] g = make_adder("hel")
    print(g())
